+#!/usr/bin/env python3
+"""Compile, link and run inputs near each guarded recursion/AST-chain limit.
+
+A clean parse is not by itself proof of robustness: semantic analysis,
+lowering, code generation, assembly and runtime must all survive the admitted
+shape. The cases below exercise the full pipeline at approximately 95% of the
+selected ceiling under the production 64 MiB parser-thread stack
+(`src/lib.rs:STACK_SIZE`). They cover recursive expression/declarator/type
+paths, iterative loops that construct deep expression ASTs, blocks,
+initializers and records.
+
+Calibration (fastbuild, x86-64, `-O0 -S`, with host assemble/link/run):
+
+  nested expression from `gen_semantic`:       1151 / measured 1212 levels
+  compound blocks (`gen_braces`):                3891 / 4096 levels
+  brace initializer (`gen_init_braces`):         31104 / 32742 levels
+  nested record + forced layout:                 1944 / 2047 levels
+  left-associated binary AST chain:             15564 / 16384 nodes
+  postfix member-access AST chain:              15564 / 16384 nodes
+  named/abstract declarator + function params:   7782 / 8192 frames
+  GNU `typeof(type-name)` recursion:               1945 / 2048 frames
+
+The first four maxima were measured against the parser budgets. The later
+95% values are explicit budget-boundary cases: each source level contributes
+a known parser frame or loop-built AST node, and the shared parser-frame
+counter protects the same native stack across parser classes. The measured
+scope is specific to these source shapes; it does not claim generic constant
+evaluation or every compiler-pipeline recursion is bounded. If any
+admitted-boundary case fails in a post-parser phase, recalibrate the relevant
+limit and document the narrower contract. A diagnostic or host-tool error
+is not a substitute for a successful compile-link-run.
+
+Usage:
+  python3 scripts/check_nesting_pipeline_boundaries.py [--lccc PATH] [--cc gcc]
+  python3 scripts/check_nesting_pipeline_boundaries.py --selftest
